2. Implementation and Design Pattern
2.1 Design Patterns
1. Singleton Pattern
The Singleton pattern was used to manage database connections efficiently. This ensures that only one database connection instance is maintained throughout the application lifecycle.
Justification:
The Singleton pattern improves resource management and prevents unnecessary multiple database connections.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert Singleton Pattern Backend Code Screenshot]

2. Factory Pattern
The Factory pattern was implemented to generate license request objects dynamically.
Justification:
The Factory pattern improves flexibility and reduces code duplication when creating different request objects.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert Factory Pattern Backend Code Screenshot]

3. MVC Pattern
The project follows the Model-View-Controller architecture.
Models manage database schemas
Controllers manage business logic
Views/frontend manage user interface
Justification:
MVC improves maintainability, scalability, and separation of concerns.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert MVC Structure Screenshot]

4. Middleware Pattern
Middleware was implemented in Express.js to manage authentication and authorization.
Justification:
Middleware centralizes request validation and security logic.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert Middleware Code Screenshot]

5. Observer Pattern
The Observer pattern was used for handling notifications and request status updates.
Justification:
The Observer pattern allows automatic updates when request status changes occur.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert Observer Pattern Code Screenshot]

