6.2 Load Testing and Performance Analysis
Load testing was conducted using Apache Benchmark (ab) to evaluate application performance under concurrent user requests.
Baseline Load Test
The first load test was conducted using lower concurrency.
Example command:
ab -n 100 -c 10 http://<ALB-DNS>/

Key Metrics
Requests per second measures how many requests the server can process per second.
Time per request measures average request processing time.
Failed requests indicate unsuccessful responses.
The system demonstrated stable performance with zero failed requests.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert Apache Benchmark Baseline Test Screenshot]

Higher Concurrency Load Test
A second test was conducted with higher concurrency and request count.
Example command:
ab -n 500 -c 50 http://<ALB-DNS>/

The second test showed slightly higher response times due to increased traffic load, but the application remained stable without failed requests.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert Higher Concurrency Apache Benchmark Screenshot]

CloudWatch Metrics
AWS CloudWatch was used to monitor CPU utilization and performance metrics during load testing.
The metrics demonstrated CPU usage spikes during benchmark execution, confirming that the servers processed increased workloads successfully.
[PLACEHOLDER – Insert CloudWatch CPU Utilization Screenshot]

Brief Analysis
The AWS Application Load Balancer successfully distributed incoming traffic between two EC2 instances using round-robin load balancing. During load testing with Apache Benchmark, the system maintained stable performance with zero failed requests and acceptable response times even under higher concurrency levels. CloudWatch metrics showed temporary CPU utilisation spikes during stress testing, demonstrating that the infrastructure responded appropriately to increased demand. The implementation improved application reliability and availability by preventing a single point of failure while also providing a cost-effective cloud deployment solution using lightweight EC2 instances and managed AWS services.

